#!/usr//bin/env raku

=begin pod

=comment This manual was remastered by merging independent draft proposals
=comment written by Claude and Codex, each checked against the bin/onefite
=comment source and the live `onefite --help` usage output, then reconciled
=comment into this single version.

=NAME

onefite - Create, fit, plot, convert, archive, and serve OneFit-Engine models

=SYNOPSIS

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite [-h|--help]
onefite [-v|--version]
onefite man [-m|--man]

onefite create FUNCTION [DATA-FILE ...] [OPTIONS]
onefite fit FUNCTION [DATA-FILE ...] [OPTIONS]
onefite fit INPUT-FILE [OPTIONS]
onefite random FUNCTION [DATA-FILE ...] [OPTIONS]
onefite plot INPUT-FILE [OPTIONS]

onefite list [models|aliases] [--quiet]
onefite help MODEL [KEY] [--quiet]
onefite convert INPUT-FILE OUTPUT-FILE [--work-folder=DIR]

onefite archive [--fit=ENTRY] [EXTRA-OPTION ...]
onefite ar      [--fit=ENTRY] [EXTRA-OPTION ...]
onefite log     [--fit=ENTRY] [EXTRA-OPTION ...]
onefite path [PATH-OPTION ...]

onefite service start [OPTIONS]
onefite service stop
onefite service log
onefite service PID
onefite start-web-engine [OPTIONS]
onefite stop-web-engine

onefite test list
onefite test [TEST ...] [OPTIONS]
onefite upgrade [OPTIONS]
onefite uninstall
=end code

=DESCRIPTION

C<onefite> is both the command-line interface and a small HTTP micro-service
for OneFit-Engine (OFE). It imports experimental data, creates engine
descriptions, compiles user-defined model functions, performs individual,
global, or hybrid/mixed nonlinear least-squares fits using CERNLIB MINUIT,
produces plots (via Grace/xmgrace, as PDF, optionally animated to MPEG4), and
optionally archives or packages the results.

A fit can be started from a model expression and one or more data files:

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a,b)=a+b*x' data.dat
=end code

It can also be started from an existing OneFit-Engine C<.json> or C<.sav>
description:

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit experiment.json
=end code

Unless another directory is selected, temporary fit files are written beneath
the current directory. Informational messages are normally written to standard
error; command results are written to standard output. Successful fits can
optionally be recorded to a local archive/log for later lookup (see
C<archive>/C<ar>/C<log> below).

=COMMANDS

=head2 create

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite create FUNCTION [DATA-FILE ...] [OPTIONS]
=end code

Parse a model expression and its data files, compile the generated model code,
and emit or save an OFE description I<without running a fit>. If
C<--save-to> is omitted, the generated JSON is written to standard output.
C<create> is the only command that understands C<--ist-ffc-Mz>; it has no
C<--no-plot>, C<--archive>, C<--zip-to>, or fit-quality options (C<--hybrid>,
C<--mp4>, C<--print-cols>, C<--R2>, C<--reduced-chi2>, C<--error-bars>,
C<--parallel>, C<--workers>, C<--remove-outliers>, C<--ramdisk>) since no fit
is actually performed.

=head2 fit

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit FUNCTION [DATA-FILE ...] [OPTIONS]
onefite fit INPUT-FILE [OPTIONS]
=end code

Run a fit from either a model expression or an existing C<.json>/C<.sav>
engine file. Most options below are shared between the two forms - see
"COMMON OPTIONS" for exactly which ones apply to which form.

The expression form imports the supplied data, resolves model aliases,
compiles generated code, runs the selected MINUIT methods, and optionally
saves, archives, or packages the result. If the expression contains a
parameter declaration ending in C<.MIXED=1>, global mode is turned on
automatically (this automatic detection applies only to the expression form,
not to the input-file form below).

The input-file form reloads an existing engine description; it understands
C<--export> and C<--individual>, which the expression form does not, but does
I<not> understand the data-import options (C<--data-label>, C<--gfilt>,
C<--fit-if>, C<--plot-if>, C<--range>, C<--R1>, C<--set-err>, C<--SymbSize>,
C<--sef-R1-file>, C<--tags>, C<--aux-code>) since the definition already has
them baked in. By default, it also creates a ZIP archive whose name is
derived from INPUT-FILE. Use C<--zip-to> to select another archive name.

=head2 random

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite random FUNCTION [DATA-FILE ...] [OPTIONS]
=end code

Assign random initial values within each bounded parameter range, mark those
parameters fixed, and run the resulting model. C<random> has no
C<--fit-methods>, C<--aux-code>, C<--archive>, or C<#>-prefixed dynamic
override support, and its "skip plotting" flag is spelled C<--inp>/
C<--no-plot> rather than the C<-np>/C<--no-plot> used by C<fit>.

=head2 plot

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite plot INPUT-FILE [OPTIONS]
=end code

Render plots from an existing C<.json> or C<.sav> file without fitting. The
command creates a work directory named after INPUT-FILE and packages the plot
results. C<plot> only understands the auto-scale/log-scale flags,
C<--Num>/C<--npts>, and C<--work-folder> - it has no C<--quiet>,
C<--save-to>, or any fit-quality option.

=head2 convert

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite convert INPUT-FILE OUTPUT-FILE [--wf|--work-folder=DIR]
=end code

Convert between OneFit-Engine C<.json> files and fitteia C<.sav> files
(direction is inferred from the two file extensions given). The default
working directory is C</tmp>.

=head2 list

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite list [--quiet]
onefite list models [--quiet]
onefite list aliases [--quiet]
=end code

With no selector, print both installed model metadata and aliases. C<models>
prints C<C/META-C.json>. C<aliases> merges the installed C<aliases.json> with
a local C<./aliases.json>; local entries take precedence.

=head2 help

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite help MODEL [--quiet]
onefite help MODEL KEY [--quiet]
=end code

Print all metadata for MODEL, or only the value associated with KEY. If MODEL
is not found the command still exits successfully and reports a "not found"
message instead.

=head2 archive, ar, and log

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite archive
onefite archive --fit=ENTRY [EXTRA-OPTION ...]
=end code

C<archive>, C<ar>, and C<log> are three names for the same command. Without
C<--fit>, they print the fit history: one 0-indexed line per entry (index,
timestamp, archive filename and size, and the original command). With
C<--fit=ENTRY>, they unzip that entry's archived data and re-run the exact
original command against it. ENTRY may be that printed index, or C<last-N>
to count back N entries from the most recent; any other value (including the
literal word C<last>) falls back to the most recent entry. Any additional,
otherwise-unrecognised named option is appended verbatim to the re-run
command, which lets you replay a stored fit with extra flags applied:

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite archive --fit=last
onefite ar -f last-1 --rchi2
onefite log -f 2 --ro=1
=end code

=head2 path

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite path [-s|--src] [-t] [--exa|--examples] [-b|--bin] [-l|--lib]
             [-c|-C] [--log] [-a|--aliases] [--ar|--archive]
=end code

Print installed paths. With no selector, print all known paths.

=item C<-s>, C<--src> - OneFit-Engine source directory.
=item C<-t> - Raku test directory (F<t/>). Short option only; there is no C<--t> long form.
=item C<--exa>, C<--examples> - Examples directory.
=item C<-b>, C<--bin> - Installed executable directory.
=item C<-l>, C<--lib> - Raku/C library directory (holds the compiled C/Fortran static libs).
=item C<-c>, C<-C> - C and Fortran source directory. Both are short options; there is no C<--C> long form.
=item C<--log> - Log directory (holds the running service's log file).
=item C<-a>, C<--aliases> - Configuration directory containing installed C<aliases.json>.
=item C<--ar>, C<--archive> - Fit archive-log directory.

Examples

=begin code :lang<bash>
cd $(onefite path -s)
vi $(onefite path -a)/aliases.json
cat $(onefite path --log)/onefite
=end code

=head2 service

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite service start [--ip=ADDRESS] [-p|--port=PORT] [-d|--systemd-daemon]
                      [--rd|--ram|--ramdisk|--RAMDisk|--use-ramdisk]
onefite service stop
onefite service log
onefite service PID
=end code

Start or manage the OFE HTTP service. The default bind address is C<0.0.0.0>
and the default port is C<8142>. Bind to C<127.0.0.1> when the service should
only be reachable from the local machine (see SECURITY below).

C<--systemd-daemon> reloads systemd, enables C<onefite.service>, and starts
the service. Without it, the service is started in the background (from
C<$HOME/public_html>) by killing any already-running instance and relaunching
C<start-web-engine> under C<nohup>. C<--use-ramdisk> copies
C<$HOME/public_html> onto a fresh RAM disk (backing the original up to
C<$HOME/public_html_bak> first) before starting the service.

C<service log> shows the service log file; C<service PID> prints its process
id (found via C<ps aux | grep start-web-engine>).

=head2 start-web-engine and stop-web-engine

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite start-web-engine [--ip[=ADDRESS]] [-p|--port[=PORT]]
onefite stop-web-engine
(OBSOLETE) onefite start-engine [--np|--no-plot] [-q|--quiet] [--wf|--work-folder=DIR]
=end code

Run the HTTP engine directly, in the foreground - what C<service start>/
C<stop> ultimately drive. If C<--ip> is omitted it is auto-detected from the
machine's first IPv4 address (falling back to C<127.0.0.1>). See "HTTP API"
below for the routes it serves. C<stop-web-engine> stops the process matching
C<start-web-engine>.

C<start-engine> is an older, obsolete alternative: instead of an HTTP server
it watches the current directory for C<.sav>/C<.json> files (optionally
prefixed C<__fit__>/C<__plot__>/C<__fit-only__> to pick the action) and
fits/plots each as it appears, in an infinite loop. Prefer the HTTP service
or the plain C<fit>/C<plot> commands instead.

=head2 HTTP API

C<start-web-engine> (and therefore C<service start>) serves a small Cro-based
HTTP application on C<--ip>:C<--port>. Every request is appended to the
service log together with the requesting host and, for C</fit> and
C</convert>, the time taken.

=item C<GET /> - a minimal HTML upload form (useful for smoke-testing the
service from a browser); it posts to C</fit> or C</convert>.
=item C<GET>/C<POST> C</list>, C</list/models>, C</list/aliases> -
C<text/plain> output identical to C<onefite list [models|aliases] --quiet>.
=item C<GET>/C<POST> C</help/MODEL[/KEY]> - C<text/plain> output identical to
C<onefite help MODEL [KEY] --quiet>. The C<POST> form takes multipart fields
C<model> (required) and, optionally, one further field whose I<value> is
used as KEY (any field name works; only its value is inspected).
=item C<GET>/C<POST> C</man> - C<text/plain> body containing the troff/groff
rendering of this manual (what C<Pod::To::Man> produces), not plain text.
=item C<POST /fit> - multipart upload. Required field C<file> (a C<.dat>/
C<.txt>/C<.zip>/C<.hdf5>/... data file). All other fields are optional and
mirror the C<fit FUNCTION ...> command-line options under the same short or
long names, including C<function>, C<fit-methods>/C<fm>, C<Num>/C<npts>,
C<SymbSize>/C<ssz>, C<global>/C<g>, C<hybrid>/C<mixed>, C<mpeg4>/C<mp4>,
C<autox>/C<autoy>/C<autoxy>, C<logx>/C<logy>/C<logxy> (and their aliases),
C<reduced-chi2>/C<rchi2>, C<R1>, C<R2>/C<pearson-correlation>,
C<sef-R1-file>, C<print-cols>, C<err>/C<set-err>, C<range>, C<remove-outliers>,
C<fit-if>, C<plot-if>, C<gfilt>, C<username> (per-user work folder), and any
C<#...>-prefixed field for a dynamic parameter override. Boolean-style
fields are truthy on C<"yes"> or C<1>. A C<download> field selects the
response: C<"zip"> streams back the whole work folder as a zip
(C<application/octet-stream>); C<"json"> streams back the saved fit
definition (C<FOLDER/BASENAME.json>, where FOLDER and BASENAME derive from
the uploaded file's own name); any other non-empty value is treated as a
path relative to that same per-upload folder and streamed back verbatim;
when C<download> is absent, the response is the C<text/plain> fit log
instead. The temporary per-request work folder is deleted once the response
has been sent.
=item C<POST /convert> - multipart upload, required field C<file> (a
C<.sav> or C<.json> file). Converts it to the other format and streams the
result back as C<application/octet-stream>.

=head2 test

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite test list
onefite test [TEST ...] [--verbose|-v] [-d|--systemd-daemon] [-p|--post-install]
=end code

C<test list> lists the available C<.rakutest> files by name, without running
them. C<test> with no file arguments runs the full suite via C<prove6 --lib
t/>; given one or more TEST names (with or without the C<.rakutest> suffix),
only those are run and their combined output is captured and printed.
Systemd-daemon and post-install checks are enabled by default; disable them
with C<--no-systemd-daemon>/C<--no-post-install> when inappropriate for the
current platform. C<--post-install> always fits a small built-in dataset via
the plain command line and checks the result. If C<--systemd-daemon> is set
and C<systemctl status onefite> reports it active, it also fits the same
dataset by C<curl>-ing the running HTTP service on C<127.0.0.1:8142>;
otherwise, if a background service process is found (C<onefite service
PID>), it curls that instead; if neither is running, the HTTP check is
skipped with a note that the service is off, and only the command-line check
runs.

=head2 upgrade

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite upgrade [-u|--to-user] [-c|--compile] [-d|--systemd-daemon]
                [--cernlib] [--shell] [--shell-port=PORT]
                [--ip=ADDRESS] [-p|--port=PORT] [--test]
                [-m|--merge-site=BRANCH] [--minuit=COUNT]
                [--wsl|--macos|--docker] [--apt|--apt-get|--dpkg] [--git]
=end code

Update the current checkout (C<git stash && git pull>, unless C<--no-git>)
and invoke C<INSTALL> with the corresponding options, then restart the
service. C<--minuit> defaults to whatever the currently-built MINUIT already
supports (read back from its own compiled-in F<d506cm.inc>) rather than a
fixed number. Git update, compilation, and package update are enabled by
default. Boolean options can be negated with C<--no-NAME> or C<--/NAME>.

=head2 uninstall

Stop the web engine, update the source checkout, and run C<INSTALL -U>. This
removes the installed OneFit-Engine components and may require elevated
privileges.

=MODEL EXPRESSIONS

A model expression has this general form:

=begin code
OUTPUT[OUTPUT-RANGE](X[X-RANGE], PARAMETER, ...) = EXPRESSION
=end code

Parameters may be written as follows:

=item C<a> - free parameter with engine-selected initialization
=item C<a:1.0> - free parameter initialized to 1.0
=item C<< a[0<10] >> - free parameter bounded to the inclusive range 0 through 10
=item C<< a:1.0[0<10] >> - initialized and bounded parameter
=item C<a=1.0> - fixed parameter
=item C<< a=1.0[0<10] >> - fixed parameter that still carries an allowed range
=item C<< a=1.0[0<10].MIXED=1 >> - per-block parameter in a C<--hybrid> fit (see FITTING MODES)

Both C<< '<' >> and C<';'> are accepted as range separators, though
C<< '<' >> usually requires less escaping inside a quoted shell argument.

A backslash before C<+> marks separately-plotted model contributions:

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a,b,c)=a\+b*x+c*x*x' data.dat
=end code

Quote the complete expression to prevent the shell from interpreting spaces,
C<< '<' >>, C<#>, C<*>, parentheses, or backslashes.

=ALIASES

Installed aliases are read from the installation C<aliases.json>. A local
C<./aliases.json> is merged over the installed file, with local entries
taking precedence. Aliases can be selected in several equivalent forms:

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'alias: one exponential' data.dat
onefite fit 'a: one exponential' data.dat
onefite fit '#one-exponential' data.dat
=end code

Use C<--define-alias=NAME> (C<-a>/C<--da>/C<--dali>; also C<--alias> on the
expression form of C<fit>) after fitting to create or replace a local alias
with the fitted function.

=FITTING MODES

=item Individual - fit each data set independently. This is the default
unless the engine file or command selects another mode; C<-i>/C<--individual>
forces this mode, and is only available when loading an existing engine file
(C<fit INPUT-FILE>).
=item Global - C<-g>/C<--global> minimizes a shared objective across all
supplied data sets. Available on C<create>, C<random>, and both forms of
C<fit>.
=item Hybrid or MIXED - C<--hybrid> enables mixed fitting and also implies
global mode. It is only available on C<fit> (either form); C<create> and
C<random> do not support it. A function expression containing a C<.MIXED=1>
parameter declaration automatically activates global mode too, but only when
fitting from a function expression directly (C<fit FUNCTION ...>), not when
re-fitting a saved C<INPUT-FILE>. Parameters tagged C<.MIXED=1> are fitted
per data set while the remaining (untagged) parameters participate in the
shared global fit.

=INPUT FILES

=item C<.dat>, C<.txt> - plain-text experimental data
=item C<.zip> - archive containing input data
=item C<.sdf>, C<.hdf5> - Stelar data
=item C<.sef> - Stelar field-cycling data
=item C<.sav> - fitteia/OneFit-Engine description
=item C<.json> - OneFit-Engine description

The import options C<--R1>, C<--sef-R1-file>, C<--range>, C<--gfilt>,
C<--fit-if>, C<--plot-if>, C<--ist-ffc-Mz>, and C<--set-err> customize
conversion of supported experimental formats; all of them apply only when
building a definition from raw data (C<create> and C<fit FUNCTION ...>), not
when re-fitting or re-plotting a saved C<INPUT-FILE>.

=COMMON OPTIONS

Each item below notes, in parentheses, which commands accept it: C<create>,
C<random>, C<fit-expr> (C<fit FUNCTION ...>), C<fit-file> (C<fit INPUT-FILE>),
and C<plot>.

=head2 Data import and model creation

=item C<--dl>, C<--data-label>, C<--data-labels=TEXT> - supply labels and
values used while importing data blocks. I<(create, random; fit-expr also
has this option, but without the C<-dl> short alias)>
=item C<-t>, C<--tags=TEXT> - assign data-block tags. I<(create, random, fit-expr)>
=item C<--R1> - import R1 rather than Mz data from supported IST-FFC and
Stelar files. I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--ist-ffc-Mz>, C<--ist-ffc> - import Mz (rather than R1) data from an
IST-FFC file. I<(create only)>
=item C<--sf>, C<--sef-R1-file=FILE> - read frequencies from FILE while
importing Stelar SEF data. I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<-r>, C<--range=TEXT> - select a range when importing Stelar data
zones. I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--gfilt=INTEGER> - apply a Gaussian smoothing filter during import.
I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--fi>, C<--fit-if=CONDITION> - fit only data blocks whose tags
satisfy CONDITION. I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--pi>, C<--plot-if=CONDITION> - plot only data blocks and fitted
curves whose tags satisfy CONDITION. I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--se>, C<--set-err>, C<--err=EXPRESSION> - set or derive errors for
ordinary data files. Supported expressions include a constant, a percentage,
a multiplier, standard deviation, averaging, and splitting around a value -
for example C<1.0>, C<1%>, C<10x>, C<x10/100>, C<std>, C<std split at 5>, or
C<10% avg split at 10.5>. Does not apply to IST-FFC, SEF, or HDF5 imports
(their errors come from the instrument format itself). I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--ac>, C<--aux-code=SPECIFICATION> - add auxiliary C source and
declarations. SPECIFICATION is a comma-separated string whose first value is
the C source filename and whose remaining values are declarations, for
example C<< --aux-code='functions.c, double custom(double x)' >>.
I<(create, fit-expr)>

=head2 Fit control

These only apply once an actual MINUIT fit is performed, so I<not> to
C<create> or C<plot>.

=item C<--fm>, C<--fit-methods=METHODS> - whitespace-separated MINUIT
methods, run in order. Default: C<simp scan min minos>. I<(create, fit-expr,
fit-file; C<random> has no C<--fit-methods> at all - it reuses whatever the
parsed function/definition already carries)>
=item C<-g>, C<--global>; C<-i>, C<--individual>; C<--hybrid> - select the
fitting mode (see FITTING MODES above for exact applicability).
=item C<--ro>, C<--remove-outliers=SELECTION> - remove selected outliers, for
example C<2>, C<2..5>, or C<1,3,5>. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<--rc>, C<--rchi2>, C<--reduced-chi2> - recalculate parameter errors
for a chi-square approximately equal to the number of degrees of freedom.
I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<--eb>, C<--error-bars>, C<--errorbars> - include error bars in fit
output and plots. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<--pco>, C<--peco>, C<--pearson-correlation>, C<--R2> - calculate the
Pearson correlation quality coefficient. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<--parallel>, C<--no-parallel> - enable or disable parallel execution
of hybrid/mixed fits (enabled by default). Disabling it sets
C<ONEFITE_NO_PARALLEL=1> for the process. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<--workers=INTEGER> - set the worker count for mixed-fit parallelism.
Positive values set C<ONEFITE_WORKERS> for the process. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<--ar>, C<--archive> - after a successful fit, append the exact
command line to the local fit archive/log. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>

=head2 Plot control

=item C<--ax>, C<--autox>; C<--ay>, C<--autoy>; C<--axy>, C<--autoxy> -
auto-scale the x axis, y axis, or both. I<(all commands)>
=item C<--lx>, C<--logx>, C<--loglin>; C<--ly>, C<--logy>, C<--linlog>;
C<--lxy>, C<--logxy>, C<--loglog> - logarithmic x, y, or both axes.
I<(all commands)>
=item C<-n>, C<--Num>, C<--npts=INTEGER> - number of points in theoretical
curves. Default: 100. I<(create, random, plot; C<fit-expr>/C<fit-file> also
accept C<--num> and C<-N> as extra aliases for the same option)>
=item C<--SymbSize>, C<--ssz=NUMBER> - non-negative plot-symbol size.
Default: C<1.0>. I<(create, fit-expr)>
=item C<--np>, C<--no-plot> - run without producing plots. I<(fit-expr,
fit-file; C<random> has the same option spelled C<--inp>/C<--no-plot>
instead; C<create> never plots and C<plot> always does, so neither has this
flag)>
=item C<--mp4>, C<--mpeg4> - create C<All.mp4> from C<All.pdf> via the
installed C<pdf2mp4> helper. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>

=head2 Output and workspace

=item C<-o>, C<--st>, C<--save-to=FILE> - save settings and fit results as a
C<.json> or C<.sav> file. I<(create, random, fit-file - C<--to> is an extra
alias on these three; C<fit-expr> has the same option but without the
C<--to> alias; C<plot> has no C<--save-to>)>
=item C<-z>, C<--zt>, C<--zip-to=FILE> - package the working result
directory as FILE. I<(random, fit-expr, fit-file - on fit-file it defaults
to INPUT-FILE's own basename; C<create> never zips)>
=item C<--pc>, C<--prco>, C<--print-cols>, C<--print-columns>, C<--cols=LIST>
- select fit-results columns by index or parameter name, after fitting. A
suffix such as C<a+1> or C<a-1> selects a column relative to parameter C<a>.
I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<-e>, C<--export> - when loading an engine file, export its data to
C<data-BASENAME.txt> and any auxiliary source to C<AuxCode-BASENAME.c>.
I<(fit-file only)>
=item C<--wf>, C<--work-folder=DIR> - parent of generated work directories.
Default is the current directory, except for C<convert>, which defaults to
C</tmp>. I<(all commands)>
=item C<--rd>, C<--ram>, C<--ramdisk>, C<--RAMDisk>, C<--use-ramdisk> - stage
the work folder on a RAM disk instead of local disk. I<(fit-expr, fit-file)>
=item C<-q>, C<--quiet> - reduce informational log output; does not suppress
the underlying result. I<(create, random, fit-expr, fit-file; C<plot> has no
C<--quiet>)>

=head2 Dynamic model overrides

Named options beginning with C<#> update parts of the model expression (or a
saved definition) before fitting. Quote these arguments to prevent shell
comment and glob processing. I<(create, fit-expr, fit-file; not available on
C<random> or C<plot>)>

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a,b)=a+b*x' data.dat '--#a=0.0[0<2]' '--#x[0<10]'
=end code

Option names not recognised as either a declared parameter/axis or one of
the flags above print a warning ("...not defined...continue...") and are
otherwise ignored, rather than aborting the run.

=FILES

Use C<onefite path> to discover installation-dependent paths. Important
files include the installed C<C/META-C.json>, the installed C<aliases.json>,
an optional local C<./aliases.json>, the service log (C<onefite path --log>),
and the fit archive directory (C<onefite path --archive>).

=ENVIRONMENT

=item C<ONEFITE_NO_PARALLEL> - set to C<1> internally by C<--no-parallel> on
C<onefite fit>. Read by the compiled C fitting core to disable parallel
(OpenMP) fitting of hybrid/mixed data blocks.
=item C<ONEFITE_WORKERS> - set internally by a positive C<--workers=N> on
C<onefite fit>. Read by the same C fitting core to cap the number of
parallel fitting workers used for hybrid/mixed fits.

=EXIT STATUS

The command returns zero after successful completion. Invalid input, missing
files or aliases, generated-code compilation errors, and fitting errors
return a nonzero status. Some administrative commands delegate their status
to external programs (C<git>, C<systemctl>, C<zef>, ...).

=SECURITY

C<start-web-engine> accepts uploaded model and data files and compiles and
executes the resulting generated C code. Bind it to C<127.0.0.1> unless
remote access is deliberately required and protected by appropriate firewall
and access controls.

C<upgrade>, C<uninstall>, and systemd service management invoke C<git>,
installation scripts, and other external commands, and in some modes
C<sudo>. Review their effects before using them on production systems.

Fit, plot, service, and RAM-disk operations remove and recreate working
directories (C<rm -fr> the target folder before writing to it). Do not point
C<--work-folder> at a directory containing unrelated files under a name that
a generated result would reuse.

=EXAMPLES

=head2 Fit a straight line

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a:0,b:1)=a+b*x' data.dat
=end code

=head2 Use bounded and fixed parameters

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y[0<10](x[0<5],a:1[0<10],b=2)=a+b*x' data.dat
=end code

=head2 Save results and create an archive

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit '#1exp' data.txt --save-to=fit.json --zip-to=fit.zip --archive
=end code

=head2 Refit an existing description

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit fit.json --fit-methods='simp scan min' --autoxy
=end code

=head2 Run a global or hybrid fit

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a,b)=a+b*x' sample-*.dat --global --workers=2
onefite fit '#2exp' sample.hdf5 --hybrid --workers=2
=end code

=head2 Set errors and remove outliers

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a,b)=a+b*x' data.dat \
    --set-err='10% avg split at 10.5' --remove-outliers='1,3,5'
=end code

=head2 Select result columns

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite fit 'y(x,a,b)=a+b*x' data.dat --print-columns='2,a,a+1'
=end code

=head2 Create a definition once, fit it later

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite create 'y(x,a,b) = a + b*x' data.dat --save-to=fit-def.json
onefite fit fit-def.json --export
onefite fit fit-def.json --individual --fit-methods='simp scan min'
=end code

=head2 Inspect a model and installed paths

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite list models
onefite help BPP
onefite path --aliases
=end code

=head2 Start a local-only HTTP service

=begin code :lang<bash>
onefite service start --ip=127.0.0.1 --port=8142
=end code

=DIAGNOSTICS

If an alias is not found, C<onefite> prints the installed and local alias
locations and the available keys. Use C<onefite list aliases> to inspect
them.

If generated C code fails to compile, inspect C<compile.log> in the
generated work directory. For undeclared helper functions, provide their
implementation and declarations with C<--aux-code>.

If service startup fails, inspect C<onefite service log>, verify the bind
address exists, and check whether the port is already in use or blocked by a
firewall.

=SEE ALSO

C<onefite --help>, C<onefite man>, C<onefite list models>,
C<onefite help MODEL>, L<https://github.com/fitteia/OneFit-Engine>

=

my $time = BEGIN { now }
END { note "===> Total execution time (s): " , now - INIT now }

note "===> Compile time: ", now - $time;

our constant OFE-PATH="%OFE-PATH%";

use OneFit;
use OneFit::Engine::Grammars;
